# Explicit Intent & AI Readability

## Purpose

Write code so that its intent can be understood without reverse-engineering
the implementation.

## Rules

- Prefer explicit code over clever or indirect implementations.
- Prefer descriptive names over abbreviated or ambiguous names.
- Prefer simple control flow over deeply nested logic.
- Prefer named intermediate values when they clarify business meaning.
- Extract non-trivial business rules into named functions.
- Avoid unnecessary abstraction layers that only forward calls.
- Avoid complex boolean expressions when named predicates improve clarity.
- Avoid compressed one-liners when a straightforward implementation is clearer.
- Do not optimize for fewer lines at the expense of readability.
- Preserve clear existing abstractions unless there is a concrete reason to change them.

## AI Readability

Code should minimize the reasoning required to reconstruct intent.

When modifying code:

- Keep business rules close to the behavior they control.
- Keep unrelated concerns separate.
- Prefer structures that allow an agent to locate intent without reading
  unrelated implementation details.
- Prefer explicit data flow over implicit side effects.
- Prefer predictable control flow over clever abstractions.

## Do Not

Do not introduce:

- abstraction solely to reduce line count
- wrappers that only forward calls
- generic helpers with unclear ownership
- deeply nested conditional logic
- ambiguous variable or function names
- compressed expressions that hide business rules

## Out of scope

`explicit-intent` MUST NOT define:

- where to search for code
- how much code to read
- when to stop
- when to ask for confirmation
- which skill owns the task

Those concerns belong to `agent-ops`, `enterprise-safety`, and `skill-router`.
See `rules/README.md` (Rule Boundaries).

## Principle

Prefer code that explains itself over code that requires explanation.
